# The kernel x64 runs, and the source it is built from.
#
# x64 used to take Debian's linux-image-amd64: 108 MB installed, 4230 modules,
# and -- the reason this file exists -- no CONFIG_DM_INIT, so `dm-mod.create=`
# on the kernel command line was ignored and a klibc shell script in an
# initramfs had to re-parse that command line and call veritysetup itself. That
# was a second implementation of the one boot contract, and it made every x64
# image unbootable for the length of one commit while cx3576 stayed green.
# PLAN-073 has the measurements and the decision.

# Pinned to the release Debian 13 also ships, for the reason
# pkgs/rauc/versions.env pins rauc to Debian's: moving off the distribution
# package should change exactly one thing at a time -- how the kernel is built
# and configured, not which kernel runs. v6.12.108 was the newest 6.12 stable
# tag when this was written; taking it would have made a boot failure ambiguous
# between the configuration and the version. Bumping is this file's job, and a
# bump is expected to move config/x64.config with it.
KERNEL_VERSION=v6.12.107

# `git archive` of the tag, sha256. The TREE is hashed, not a tarball: a
# release tarball can be re-rolled and a tag can be moved, while the archive is
# a deterministic byte stream over the source that is actually compiled. Same
# discipline as pkgs/rauc/versions.env, deliberately not a second one.
#
# To bump: change the tag, set the hash to the literal string PENDING, and run
# `make x64-kernel`. The build prints the hash it computed and fails; paste it
# in and run again. There is no environment variable that softens that into a
# warning -- recording a hash is an act rather than a copy from a page nobody
# re-checked.
KERNEL_SHA256=ee126cabb1ce99474d96a1af42458112f16fe3b83a2a7fde581849272b7de74d
